  message: "Fixed in PR #403 (dev 1aff4e7d51). Module-level function aliases (f = g, incl. imported g via 'from x import f; _f = f') now resolve calls straight to the target function instead of dead-ending at the kind=variable alias node. Root cause: the LHS is extracted as kind=variable, so callers of _f() got a calls edge to that 0-out-degree variable and the target appeared uncalled. Fix: py.py builds a module-scope (tree.body) alias map name->target and _process_call retargets an alias-variable callee to the real function. Production-path test evidence (test_py_function_alias.py): caller of '_is_test_path = is_test_path' now emits calls->is_test_path (was ->variable) and is_test_path is genuinely called; cross-file imported form resolves via run_behavior_map. Scoped module-level to avoid a function-local/module-variable name collision (adversarial-probe-caught, locked by regression test). Additive on the identity:F1/F4a scope-stack substrate. Both packages 100% coverage; CI self-tree-validation green."  # b308
